Markdown Content:
{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":116144,"name":"zc Liu","username":"zc_Liu","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discourse.openrobotics.org/zc_liu/{size}/31617_2.png","created_at":"2026-07-27T12:44:24.495Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003e[Update] Thanks to the great feedback in the comments regarding \u003ccode\u003erosbag2 snapshot\u003c/code\u003e, I have updated the original post to clarify how YERP and \u003ccode\u003erosbag2 snapshot\u003c/code\u003e work perfectly together as a trigger-and-capture pair!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eHi everyone, Recently I have been thinking about a very practical debugging problem in ROS / ROS2 perception pipelines, especially for AMRs and mobile robots. When a robot behaves strangely in the field, the usual workflow is often:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003erosbag record\u003c/code\u003e everything.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eBring the massive data back.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eReplay it and try to find what happened.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOf course, \u003ccode\u003erosbag2\u003c/code\u003e is powerful. I am also aware of \u003cstrong\u003erosbag2 snapshot mode\u003c/strong\u003e, which can keep recent messages in memory and dump a raw topic window when triggered. That is actually very close to the architecture I have in mind. I do not want to replace \u003ccode\u003erosbag2\u003c/code\u003e or its snapshot mode. Instead, I want to explore a lightweight layer \u003cem\u003eabove\u003c/em\u003e it: \u003cstrong\u003ean event-triggered Runtime Evidence Layer for ROS perception pipelines.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI call the current prototype \u003cstrong\u003eYERP\u003c/strong\u003e — originally “YOLO Edge Runtime Profiler”.(Project repo: \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/ZC502/yolo-edge-runtime-profiler\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003ehttps://github.com/ZC502/yolo-edge-runtime-profiler\u003c/a\u003e)*\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-116144-the-core-idea-1\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-116144-the-core-idea-1\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eThe core idea\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn my view, the layering could be:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003erosbag2 snapshot\u003c/strong\u003e: captures the raw ROS topic window.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eYERP / EvidenceFlow\u003c/strong\u003e: decides \u003cem\u003ewhen\u003c/em\u003e the snapshot is worth triggering, records \u003cem\u003ewhy\u003c/em\u003e it was triggered, and adds structured perception-runtime evidence.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSo the question is not: \u003cem\u003eCan YERP replace rosbag2?\u003c/em\u003e The question is: \u003cstrong\u003eCan YERP act as the lightweight anomaly detector / evidence sidecar layer that triggers rosbag2 snapshot at the exact right moment?\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor example, a robot may run normally most of the time, but occasionally:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eaverage FPS looks fine, but p95 / p99 latency spikes\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003ethe local planner receives perception results too late\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003ea detection frame creates too many candidates\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003epostprocess suddenly dominates runtime\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003ethe issue is hard to reproduce later\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn that case, raw topic replay is useful, but we also need to know:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003cem\u003eWhy\u003c/em\u003e was this moment captured?\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWhich frame was involved?\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWhich perception stage became slow? (preprocess, inference, postprocess, callback delay, queue delay, or message age?)\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eDid output pressure increase? Was there a box-count or candidate-count spike?\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThat is the role I am exploring for YERP.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-116144-current-prototype-status-2\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-116144-current-prototype-status-2\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eCurrent prototype status\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe current YERP Vision prototype has already been tested in a standalone YOLO / edge CV pipeline. It can monitor fields such as \u003ccode\u003epreprocess_ms\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003einference_ms\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003epostprocess_ms\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003ep50/p95/p99 latency\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003ebox count\u003c/code\u003e, and \u003ccode\u003econfidence entropy\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhen a runtime pressure event is triggered, it saves local evidence (\u003ccode\u003eimage.jpg\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003emetadata.json\u003c/code\u003e, etc.).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"lightbox-wrapper\"\u003e\u003ca class=\"lightbox\" href=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex022/uploads/ros/original/3X/1/7/17e58216189cda386d971174e4a443808b44a93f.jpeg\" data-download-href=\"/uploads/short-url/3poHrJunOcqKqFGUKitsLiuj3Tp.jpeg?dl=1\" title=\"jpg\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e\u003cimg src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex022/uploads/ros/optimized/3X/1/7/17e58216189cda386d971174e4a443808b44a93f_2_690x388.jpeg\" alt=\"jpg\" data-base62-sha1=\"3poHrJunOcqKqFGUKitsLiuj3Tp\" width=\"690\" height=\"388\" srcset=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex022/uploads/ros/optimized/3X/1/7/17e58216189cda386d971174e4a443808b44a93f_2_690x388.jpeg, https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex022/uploads/ros/optimized/3X/1/7/17e58216189cda386d971174e4a443808b44a93f_2_1035x582.jpeg 1.5x, https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex022/uploads/ros/optimized/3X/1/7/17e58216189cda386d971174e4a443808b44a93f_2_1380x776.jpeg 2x\" data-dominant-color=\"958280\"\u003e\u003cdiv class=\"meta\"\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-far-image svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#far-image\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003cspan class=\"filename\"\u003ejpg\u003c/span\u003e\u003cspan class=\"informations\"\u003e1920×1080 299 KB\u003c/span\u003e\u003csvg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-discourse-expand svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"\u003e\u003cuse href=\"#discourse-expand\"\u003e\u003c/use\u003e\u003c/svg\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn one test case, a normal-looking YOLO frame was captured not because it was manually labeled as a “\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/ZC502/yolo-edge-runtime-profiler/blob/c7d34b4da8f8048714544732c7541188f3ed14c3/assets/frame_000097_1783648890188_RED_POSTPROCESS_DOMINANT.json\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e[bad frame]\u003c/a\u003e ”, but because the runtime trace showed pressure:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-auto\"\u003e{\n  \"state\": \"RED\",\n  \"dominant_cause\": \"POSTPROCESS_DOMINANT\",\n  \"selection_reason\": \"runtime_pressure\",\n  \"metrics\": {\n    \"box_count\": 18,\n    \"class_count\": 4,\n    \"confidence_entropy\": 2.51,\n    \"preprocess_ms\": 1.20,\n    \"inference_ms\": 3.95,\n    \"postprocess_ms\": 2.02,\n    \"postprocess_ratio\": \"28.15%\"\n  }\n}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe important point is not that the image “looks abnormal”. The important point is: \u003cstrong\u003ethis input frame created measurable runtime pressure, so it became worth saving as evidence.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eCommunity Momentum\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis concept is already gaining cross-community traction:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI have introduced this EvidenceFlow approach in the \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/orgs/ultralytics/discussions/25250#discussioncomment-17677787\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003eUltralytics GitHub Discussions\u003c/a\u003e, where the team is currently gathering feedback.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI’ve also initiated an open co-testing discussion within the \u003ca href=\"https://discuss.mindspore.cn/t/topic/1566/2?u=zc502\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eMindSpore Lite\u003c/strong\u003e community\u003c/a\u003e, and developers there are exploring collaboration for edge NPU inference.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNow, I want to bring this discussion to the ROS ecosystem, which is arguably where field debugging is the most painful.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-116144-evidenceflow-schema-v01-draft-3\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-116144-evidenceflow-schema-v01-draft-3\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eEvidenceFlow Schema v0.1 Draft\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eInstead of exposing all internal YERP logic, I am thinking about a simple structured sidecar format. A ROS / ROS2 runtime pressure event could look like this:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-auto\"\u003e{\n  \"schema_version\": \"0.1\",\n  \"record_type\": \"runtime_pressure_event\",\n\n  \"sample\": {\n    \"sample_id\": \"frame_1048\",\n    \"input_ref\": \"/camera/front/image_raw\",\n    \"timestamp\": \"2026-07-27T19:32:18.104Z\"\n  },\n\n  \"environment\": {\n    \"runtime\": \"ros2\",\n    \"backend\": \"yolo_edge_runtime\",\n    \"mode\": \"event_only\",\n    \"device\": \"amr_edge_board\"\n  },\n\n  \"trigger\": {\n    \"state\": \"RED\",\n    \"reason\": \"latency_p99_spike\",\n    \"observed_ms\": 51.7,\n    \"threshold_ms\": 30.0,\n    \"dominant_cause\": \"POSTPROCESS_DOMINANT\"\n  },\n\n  \"stage_ms\": {\n    \"preprocess\": 2.1,\n    \"inference\": 15.4,\n    \"postprocess\": 34.2,\n    \"total\": 51.7\n  },\n\n  \"ros_metadata\": {\n    \"topic\": \"/camera/front/image_raw\",\n    \"node\": \"/perception/yolo_detector\",\n    \"callback_delay_ms\": null,\n    \"queue_delay_ms\": null,\n    \"message_age_ms\": null,\n    \"dropped_messages\": null\n  },\n\n  \"output_metadata\": {\n    \"box_count\": 145,\n    \"candidate_count\": 312,\n    \"confidence_entropy\": 2.8,\n    \"class_entropy\": 1.4\n  },\n\n  \"hardware_metadata\": {\n    \"cpu_usage\": null,\n    \"gpu_usage\": null,\n    \"npu_usage\": null,\n    \"memory_spike_mb\": 12,\n    \"temperature_c\": null\n  },\n\n  \"snapshot\": {\n    \"rosbag2_snapshot_triggered\": true,\n    \"window_sec\": 5\n  },\n\n  \"privacy\": {\n    \"local_first\": true,\n    \"image_saved\": true,\n    \"upload_performed\": false\n  }\n}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cem\u003eThe goal is not to force every ROS project to use these exact fields, but to discuss what a useful runtime evidence record should contain.\u003c/em\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-116144-runtime-overhead-4\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-116144-runtime-overhead-4\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eRuntime Overhead\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA natural concern is: \u003cem\u003eWill such a probe slow down the robot?\u003c/em\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\nAbsolutely valid. A probe should not become the new bottleneck. The design separates the workload:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFast path (Main inference loop):\u003c/strong\u003e read existing timestamps, update a fixed-size rolling window, check trigger conditions. \u003cstrong\u003eNo file IO, no large tensor copies, no image saving.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eSlow path (Background worker):\u003c/strong\u003e after a trigger, write JSON / image in a background worker, and \u003cem\u003eoptionally call rosbag2 snapshot service\u003c/em\u003e. Uses a bounded queue and drops evidence if full to avoid blocking inference.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor production ROS / ROS2 systems, the default modes would likely be \u003ccode\u003eshadow_mode\u003c/code\u003e (observe only) or (write only when triggered).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-116144-how-i-imagine-the-rosbag2-integration-5\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-116144-how-i-imagine-the-rosbag2-integration-5\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eHow I imagine the rosbag2 integration\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eObserve\u003c/strong\u003e: YERP observes perception timing and output metadata.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDetect\u003c/strong\u003e: YERP detects a runtime pressure event (p99 spike, queue delay, output pressure, etc.).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLog\u003c/strong\u003e: YERP writes a small EvidenceFlow JSON sidecar.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTrigger\u003c/strong\u003e: If raw replay is needed, YERP calls \u003ccode\u003erosbag2\u003c/code\u003e snapshot service.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eResult\u003c/strong\u003e: \u003ccode\u003eraw bag window\u003c/code\u003e + \u003ccode\u003estructured reason for capture\u003c/code\u003e + \u003ccode\u003eperception-stage timing\u003c/code\u003e + \u003ccode\u003eoutput metadata\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn short: \u003cstrong\u003erosbag2 snapshot tells us \u003cem\u003ewhat\u003c/em\u003e raw ROS messages were around the event. YERP / EvidenceFlow tells us \u003cem\u003ewhy\u003c/em\u003e this event was worth capturing.\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-116144-questions-for-the-ros-community-6\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-116144-questions-for-the-ros-community-6\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eQuestions for the ROS community\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI would really appreciate feedback from people who debug ROS / ROS2 robots in the field.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDoes this EvidenceFlow schema cover the information you would want when debugging ROS perception latency?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor ROS2, should such an adapter start at the image topic level, diagnostics level, or executor / callback timing level?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor AMR / mobile robot scenarios, which fields matter most? (\u003ccode\u003eframe latency\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003emessage age\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003equeue delay\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003ecallback delay\u003c/code\u003e, \u003ccode\u003eTF wait time\u003c/code\u003e, etc.)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eHow do you currently trigger rosbag2 snapshots in real robots?\u003c/strong\u003e (Manual trigger? Diagnostics threshold? Topic frequency monitoring? Custom anomaly detector? Lifecycle event? Nav2 state?)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWould a small structured JSON sidecar make snapshot bags easier to triage later?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-116144-boundary-7\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-116144-boundary-7\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eBoundary\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTo avoid misunderstanding:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYERP is not a replacement for rosbag2.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYERP is not a replacement for tracing or profilers.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYERP does not identify the final root cause by itself.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eYERP tries to capture the input frame and runtime metadata that make a case \u003cem\u003eworth investigating\u003c/em\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMy current goal is to turn field debugging from \u003cstrong\u003e“record everything and search later”\u003c/strong\u003e into \u003cstrong\u003e“capture structured evidence when runtime pressure actually happens”\u003c/strong\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI am sharing this as an early prototype and schema draft. Comments, criticism, field stories, and suggestions are very welcome!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf you find this “Runtime Evidence Layer” concept valuable for your edge AI ROS deployments, or if you are interested in co-designing the ROS2 adapter together, feel free to drop a comment below, open an issue on GitHub, or reach out to me directly!\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":5,"updated_at":"2026-07-28T07:41:02.424Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":77,"reads":61,"readers_count":60,"score":387.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":56991,"topic_slug":"yerp-rosbag2-snapshot-can-we-capture-why-a-ros-perception-latency-spike-happened","display_username":"zc Liu","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":4,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://github.com/ZC502/yolo-edge-runtime-profiler","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"GitHub - ZC502/yolo-edge-runtime-profiler: Catch YOLO tail latency, stage imbalance, postprocess spikes, and pressure-triggered hard examples from Results.speed. · GitHub","clicks":6},{"url":"https://github.com/ZC502/yolo-edge-runtime-profiler/blob/c7d34b4da8f8048714544732c7541188f3ed14c3/assets/frame_000097_1783648890188_RED_POSTPROCESS_DOMINANT.json","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"yolo-edge-runtime-profiler/assets/frame_000097_1783648890188_RED_POSTPROCESS_DOMINANT.json at c7d34b4da8f8048714544732c7541188f3ed14c3 · ZC502/yolo-edge-runtime-profiler · GitHub","clicks":0},{"url":"https://github.com/orgs/ultralytics/discussions/25250#discussioncomment-17677787","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"Idea: Local-First MLOps Loop for Privacy-Sensitive Manufacturing YOLO Users · ultralytics · Discussion #25250 · GitHub","clicks":0},{"url":"https://discuss.mindspore.cn/t/topic/1566/2?u=zc502","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"【开源共测】Benchmark 均值掩盖了 p99 尾部延迟？征集 MindSpore Lite / Ascend 运行时证据层共测伙伴 - #2，来自 YeFeng24 - MindSpore Lite推理部署 - 昇思MindSpore论坛","clicks":0},{"url":"https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/ros-news-for-the-week-of-july-27th-2026/57096","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"ROS News for the Week of July 27th, 2026","clicks":0},{"url":"https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/making-a-degraded-network-matrix-a-required-ci-gate-and-what-did-not-reproduce/57505","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"Making a degraded-network matrix a required CI gate, and what did not reproduce","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":34103,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"mentioned_users":[],"post_url":"/t/yerp-rosbag2-snapshot-can-we-capture-why-a-ros-perception-latency-spike-happened/56991/1","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_vote":false},{"id":116148,"name":"","username":"JM_ROS","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discourse.openrobotics.org/jm_ros/{size}/12964_2.png","created_at":"2026-07-27T14:47:18.279Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eAre you aware of rosbag2 snapshot mode ? This is usually the go-to solution for this problem either coupled with a manual trigger or an anamoly detector (which is basically what you are presenting here)\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":2,"post_type":1,"posts_count":5,"updated_at":"2026-07-27T14:47:18.279Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":47,"readers_count":46,"score":59.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":56991,"topic_slug":"yerp-rosbag2-snapshot-can-we-capture-why-a-ros-perception-latency-spike-happened","display_username":"","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":3}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":22772,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"mentioned_users":[],"post_url":"/t/yerp-rosbag2-snapshot-can-we-capture-why-a-ros-perception-latency-spike-happened/56991/2","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":3}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":3,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false},{"id":116165,"name":"zc Liu","username":"zc_Liu","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discourse.openrobotics.org/zc_liu/{size}/31617_2.png","created_at":"2026-07-28T01:00:07.416Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThanks for pointing this out. Yes, \u003ccode\u003erosbag2\u003c/code\u003e snapshot mode is exactly the kind of raw-data capture backend I would like YERP to work with, not replace. I see the layering more like this:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003erosbag2 snapshot: captures a bounded raw topic window after a trigger.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eYERP / EvidenceFlow: decides when the trigger is meaningful, records why it was triggered, and adds structured runtime evidence around the internal perception pipeline.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSo I completely agree with your point: \u003ccode\u003erosbag2\u003c/code\u003e snapshot mode plus an anomaly detector is the exact right pattern. The part I am trying to explore is that anomaly detector / evidence sidecar layer.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor example, \u003ccode\u003erosbag2\u003c/code\u003e snapshot can capture the raw ROS messages around an incident, but it usually does not know what happened inside the perception callback:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003epreprocess_ms\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003einference_ms\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003epostprocess_ms\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003ecallback delay\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003equeue delay\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003emessage age\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003ebox count\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003ecandidate count\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003econfidence entropy\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\u003ccode\u003ep99 latency spike\u003c/code\u003e / \u003ccode\u003epostprocess-dominant frame\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThat is where I think YERP / EvidenceFlow can sit. A possible integration could be:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eObserve\u003c/strong\u003e: YERP observes lightweight timing and output metadata inside or near the perception node.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDetect\u003c/strong\u003e: If it detects a runtime pressure event (e.g., p99 latency spike, postprocess spike, queue delay, unusually high candidate count).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eLog Evidence\u003c/strong\u003e: YERP writes a small structured JSON sidecar detailing \u003cem\u003ewhy\u003c/em\u003e the event was triggered, which node was involved, stage timing, and output metadata.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eTrigger Backend\u003c/strong\u003e: If raw topic replay is needed, YERP also calls the \u003ccode\u003erosbag2\u003c/code\u003e snapshot service to dump the recent topic window.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSo the relationship I have in mind is:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eEvidenceFlow JSON\u003c/strong\u003e tells us: \u003cem\u003eWhy\u003c/em\u003e this event is worth looking at.\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003cstrong\u003erosbag2 snapshot\u003c/strong\u003e tells us: \u003cem\u003eWhat\u003c/em\u003e raw ROS messages were around that event.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn that sense, YERP could be a lightweight trigger and indexing layer for it. This is also why I am interested in the schema design. If YERP triggers a snapshot, the small JSON sidecar could make the resulting bag much easier to triage later:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-auto\"\u003e{\n  \"trigger\": {\n    \"reason\": \"latency_p99_spike\",\n    \"observed_ms\": 51.7,\n    \"dominant_cause\": \"POSTPROCESS_DOMINANT\"\n  },\n  \"ros_metadata\": {\n    \"topic\": \"/camera/front/image_raw\",\n    \"node\": \"/perception/yolo_detector\",\n    \"queue_delay_ms\": 12.4,\n    \"message_age_ms\": 38.6\n  },\n  \"stage_ms\": {\n    \"preprocess\": 2.1,\n    \"inference\": 15.4,\n    \"postprocess\": 34.2,\n    \"total\": 51.7\n  },\n  \"snapshot\": {\n    \"rosbag2_snapshot_triggered\": true,\n    \"window_sec\": 5\n  }\n}\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThen instead of having only a heavy bag file that needs to be replayed and inspected manually, we would have: \u003ccode\u003eraw bag window\u003c/code\u003e + \u003ccode\u003estructured reason for capture\u003c/code\u003e + \u003ccode\u003eperception-stage timing\u003c/code\u003e + \u003ccode\u003eoutput-pressure metadata\u003c/code\u003e + \u003ccode\u003eoptional keyframe\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI would be very interested to hear how people currently trigger \u003ccode\u003erosbag2\u003c/code\u003e snapshots in production systems. Do you usually use manual triggers, diagnostics thresholds, topic frequency monitoring, lifecycle events, or custom anomaly detector nodes?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eMy current thinking is that YERP could start as a small event detector / EvidenceFlow node, and optionally call \u003ccode\u003erosbag2\u003c/code\u003e snapshot when raw topic replay is needed.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":3,"post_type":1,"posts_count":5,"updated_at":"2026-07-28T01:00:07.416Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":2,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":38,"readers_count":37,"score":12.6,"yours":false,"topic_id":56991,"topic_slug":"yerp-rosbag2-snapshot-can-we-capture-why-a-ros-perception-latency-spike-happened","display_username":"zc Liu","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":22772,"username":"JM_ROS","name":"","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discourse.openrobotics.org/jm_ros/{size}/12964_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":34103,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"mentioned_users":[],"post_url":"/t/yerp-rosbag2-snapshot-can-we-capture-why-a-ros-perception-latency-spike-happened/56991/3","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false},{"id":116807,"name":"Saketh","username":"Saketh","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discourse.openrobotics.org/saketh/{size}/35012_2.png","created_at":"2026-08-13T16:46:15.172Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eAnswering your question 1, the field I’d add is a capture manifest, the actual sequence number range per topic that landed in the snapshot. rosbag2 snapshot’s window is a RAM ring buffer, so what you get depends on when the trigger call lands relative to the publish stream, not just wall clock.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTwo identical triggers can come back with slightly different windows and nothing in the output says so. Your schema already has null slots circling this (queue_delay_ms, dropped_messages), a per-topic seq range would make the capture itself checkable after the fact. I build replay tooling off recordings, so what exactly landed in the bag is the part I care about. Have you hit that mismatch on real hardware yet?\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":4,"post_type":1,"posts_count":5,"updated_at":"2026-08-13T16:46:15.172Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":24,"readers_count":23,"score":24.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":56991,"topic_slug":"yerp-rosbag2-snapshot-can-we-capture-why-a-ros-perception-latency-spike-happened","display_username":"Saketh","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":37397,"hidden":false,"trust_level":0,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"mentioned_users":[],"post_url":"/t/yerp-rosbag2-snapshot-can-we-capture-why-a-ros-perception-latency-spike-happened/56991/4","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false},{"id":117140,"name":"zc Liu","username":"zc_Liu","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discourse.openrobotics.org/zc_liu/{size}/31617_2.png","created_at":"2026-08-22T04:57:45.421Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThanks, this is a very good point. I was thinking mostly in terms of the requested snapshot window, but for replay the more important thing is the actual per-topic message range that landed in the bag.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI haven’t validated this mismatch systematically on real ROS hardware yet, so I don’t want to overclaim there. But I agree that a capture manifest — per-topic first/last sequence or recorder-side index, message count, and ideally first/last timestamp — would make the snapshot itself auditable instead of inferring its contents from the trigger time alone.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI’m shifting most of my current work toward LLM deployment/quantization diagnostics, so I probably won’t implement the ROS adapter immediately, but I’ll add this explicitly to the EvidenceFlow schema notes. It’s exactly the kind of field that matters for deterministic replay tooling.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThanks for catching that.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":5,"post_type":1,"posts_count":5,"updated_at":"2026-08-22T04:57:45.421Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":4,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":7,"readers_count":6,"score":1.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":56991,"topic_slug":"yerp-rosbag2-snapshot-can-we-capture-why-a-ros-perception-latency-spike-happened","display_username":"zc Liu","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":37397,"username":"Saketh","name":"Saketh","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discourse.openrobotics.org/saketh/{size}/35012_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":34103,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"mentioned_users":[],"post_url":"/t/yerp-rosbag2-snapshot-can-we-capture-why-a-ros-perception-latency-spike-happened/56991/5","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false}],"stream":[116144,116148,116165,116807,117140]},"timeline_lookup":[[1,27],[4,10],[5,2]],"suggested_topics":[],"tags":[],"tags_descriptions":{},"fancy_title":"YERP + rosbag2 snapshot: can we capture why a ROS perception latency spike happened?","id":56991,"title":"YERP + rosbag2 snapshot: can we capture why a ROS perception latency spike happened?","posts_count":5,"created_at":"2026-07-27T12:44:24.391Z","views":258,"reply_count":2,"like_count":4,"last_posted_at":"2026-08-22T04:57:45.421Z","visible":true,"closed":false,"archived":false,"has_summary":false,"archetype":"regular","slug":"yerp-rosbag2-snapshot-can-we-capture-why-a-ros-perception-latency-spike-happened","category_id":8,"word_count":1980,"deleted_at":null,"user_id":34103,"featured_link":null,"pinned_globally":false,"pinned_at":null,"pinned_until":null,"image_url":"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex022/uploads/ros/optimized/3X/1/7/17e58216189cda386d971174e4a443808b44a93f_2_1024x576.jpeg","slow_mode_seconds":0,"draft":null,"draft_key":"topic_56991","draft_sequence":null,"unpinned":null,"pinned":false,"current_post_number":1,"highest_post_number":5,"deleted_by":null,"actions_summary":[{"id":4,"count":0,"hidden":false,"can_act":false},{"id":8,"count":0,"hidden":false,"can_act":false},{"id":10,"count":0,"hidden":false,"can_act":false},{"id":7,"count":0,"hidden":false,"can_act":false}],"chunk_size":20,"bookmarked":false,"topic_timer":null,"message_bus_last_id":129,"participant_count":3,"show_read_indicator":false,"thumbnails":[{"max_width":null,"max_height":null,"width":1920,"height":1080,"url":"https://u